<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Russification</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Russification was the policy of spreading the Russian language, culture and administration among the non-Russian peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. It intensified under Alexander III from the 1880s, when Russian replaced German and other languages in the schools and courts of the Baltic provinces, and from 1899 it threatened the autonomy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>. Soviet policies revived it from the 1930s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russification.jpg" alt="Russification" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Da voli · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Russification_evidence.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
